{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785652723,"nonce":"L15UonfmByvccLLDc0hWbsDaggg=","title":"Measured 2026-08-02 on the colima VM (Ubuntu 24.04, kernel 6.8.0-117 aarch64) with **nfs-ganesha V4.3** and nfs-utils 2.6.4, privileged containers on a /24 bridge, overlays mounted with the exact `crates/scarab-server/src/export.rs:503` option string (kernel echoed `index=on,uuid=on,nfs_export=on`). Config as ADR-0062 would generate: v4-only, Grace 20, Lease 20, RecoveryBackend fs_ng, `Access_Type=None` + per-export CLIENT.","message":"**1. Pseudo-root enumeration — CLIENT blocks filter, but the fence is NODE-grain.**\nAn unauthorized IP mounts `:/` fine and sees an EMPTY root; a direct mount of an unauthorized Pseudo and of a random wrong hex give BYTE-IDENTICAL `ENOENT` (no authz oracle — good, but version-dependent: pin the ganesha version or assert it in the substrate check). HOWEVER with two exports both pinned to one client IP, `ls /` printed BOTH hexes, and `ls -R` walks into content. **kubelet mounts, so the NFS client is the NODE** — every Export live on a node is enumerable from that node. The 256-bit path is therefore a fence against OFF-NODE attackers only; on-node the fence is which Export is bind-mounted into which Pod. Export count also leaks via the pseudo-root's link count.\n=\u003e Design rule: serve a node only the Exports it currently needs (add on assign, RemoveExport on settle).\n\n**2. Restart is a PAUSE, not an ESTALE storm — but budget 35-50s.**\n166 ops (dd + read-back, 0.2s loop) across `kill -TERM` -\u003e overlay umount/remount -\u003e restart: **0 errors**, loop survived. Stall 36.20s, of which **ganesha took 20.3s to exit** after SIGTERM (it logs \"Removing all exports\"/\"FSAL system destroyed\" within 1s, then idles). Grace 20 -\u003e `reclaim complete` -\u003e NOT IN GRACE after 10s; first successful client op ~15.8s post-restart.\n=\u003e Any Step lease/heartbeat/timeout must exceed ganesha exit + Grace_Period. Consider SIGKILL after the FSAL-destroyed log line to reclaim ~20s.\n\n**3. REFUTED: pinning `Filesystem_id` does NOT prevent post-remount ESTALE.**\nTested with the fsid pin, without it (st_dev happened to be reallocated identically), and without it with st_dev FORCED to change (decoy overlay took 182, ours came back 192): **0 errors in all three**. With `index=on,nfs_export=on` the kernel sets `uuid=on` and handles are UUID-derived, surviving st_dev churn. Keep the pin as fsid-collision/aliasing hygiene between exports, but **`uuid=on` is the load-bearing option**, and any doc claiming the pin prevents ESTALE must be corrected.\n\n**4. RemoveExport is an INSTANT FENCE, not a drain.**\nDBus call returned in **4ms** (does not block on in-flight ops); the writer's next op failed at essentially the same instant with `Stale file handle`; **zero files added and zero sizes changed under upper/ after the call returned**, out to +8s, across two runs. Sound as the settle quiesce. ONE OBLIGATION: the in-flight file was left **TORN** (1048576 of 2097152 bytes, fsync failed). The upper tree after a fence is NOT crash-consistent — either fence only when the Pod is known exited, or record the settle as \"as-of fence, last write may be partial\".\n\n**5. HIGH-SEVERITY, unplanned: a vanished server wedges the client UNKILLABLY.**\nDestroying the server container while a client held a mount put the client's init in **`D` state in `nfs4_proc_destroy_session` -\u003e `rpc_wait_bit_killable`**, permanently. SIGKILL did nothing; a TCP-refusing endpoint at the same IP did nothing; the container was unreapable by docker for **~15 minutes**. It cleared ONLY when a live NFSv4.1 server was restored **at the same address**.\n=\u003e Translated: **if the Depot disappears, step Pods hang in Terminating and cannot be killed.** Non-negotiables: never scale the Depot to zero; never let its Service IP change; a rolling update must DRAIN first (RemoveExport all, confirm unmount, then roll). Directly constrains the shard-by-fence plan: scaling a shard AWAY would wedge every Pod holding its mounts, so rebalance/scale-down needs a drain protocol, not just rerouting.\n\nSide finding: re-mounting the same `server:/path` on the same mountpoint after a lazy umount gives `mount.nfs4: Stale file handle for (null)` (cached superblock reuse) — use a fresh mountpoint or `nosharecache`.\n\n- [ ] ADR-0062 amended with all five (esp. correcting the Filesystem_id claim and recording the node-grain fence)\n- [ ] The drain-then-roll protocol specified before any ganesha code\n- [ ] Step lease/timeout floor documented against the measured 35-50s restart stall\n- [ ] Settle records that the last write may be partial (or fences only on a known-exited Pod)","files":null}]}